   "source": [
    "### Distribution Analysis\n",
    "Before applying transformations, the distributions of key variables are analyzed to identify structural patterns and potential anomalies.\n",
    "\n",
    "The distribution of listings per host is highly right-skewed in both cities, with most hosts managing a single listing and a small number controlling multiple properties. This long-tail pattern suggests potential market concentration.\n",
    "\n",
    "Price distributions also exhibit strong right skewness, with a concentration of observations at lower price levels and a long upper tail. This indicates the presence of extreme values that may distort statistical analysis.\n",
    "\n",
    "Availability patterns differ across cities: Rome shows a higher concentration of listings available throughout the year, while Copenhagen exhibits a more dispersed distribution. This suggests different usage patterns of listings across the two markets.\n",
    "\n",
    "These distributional properties justify the use of logarithmic scales for visualization and motivate the adoption of a percentile-based approach for outlier removal in subsequent steps."
   ]
  },
