    "### Key Insights from the Exploratory Analysis"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "338752c0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Before moving to formal metrics, we summarise the main patterns emerging from the exploratory analysis. These observations help interpret the structural differences between Rome and Copenhagen and provide context for the subsequent analysis.\n",
    "\n",
    "⸻\n",
    "\n",
    "I1 — Rome shows a higher level of market concentration.\n",
    "Rome has a much larger presence of professional and semi-professional hosts compared to Copenhagen. In Copenhagen, the majority of listings belong to single-listing hosts, suggesting a more decentralized and peer-to-peer market structure. In contrast, Rome shows a stronger commercial orientation, with a larger share of listings controlled by multi-listing operators.\n",
    "\n",
    "⸻\n",
    "\n",
    "I2 — Listing availability reveals different usage patterns.\n",
    "Listings in Rome remain available for a significantly larger portion of the year, indicating a more intensive use of properties for short-term rental activity. Copenhagen shows lower median availability and fewer listings active for most of the year, suggesting a more occasional hosting model.\n",
    "\n",
    "⸻\n",
    "\n",
    "I3 — Prices are higher in Copenhagen despite lower concentration.\n",
    "Median prices are higher in Copenhagen even though the market is less professionalized and less concentrated than Rome. This suggests that Airbnb prices are influenced not only by host concentration, but also by broader housing-market conditions and possible supply constraints.\n",
    "\n",
    "⸻\n",
    "\n",
    "I4 — Entire-home listings dominate in both cities.\n",
    "In both Rome and Copenhagen, most Airbnb listings consist of entire homes rather than private or shared rooms. This indicates that short-term rental activity may directly reduce the availability of housing units for long-term residents, especially in areas with high tourism demand.\n",
    "\n",
    "⸻\n",
    "\n",
    "I5 — Spatial concentration is more intense in Rome.\n",
    "Airbnb activity in Rome is strongly concentrated in central neighbourhoods such as Centro Storico, which show extremely high listing counts and strong professional-host presence. Copenhagen also exhibits spatial concentration, but at a lower scale and with less evidence of commercial clustering.\n",
    "\n",
    "⸻\n",
    "\n",
    "I6 — The two cities reflect different Airbnb market structures.\n",
    "Overall, the results suggest that Rome operates as a more commercialized short-term rental market, while Copenhagen retains stronger characteristics of a peer-to-peer hosting system. These differences likely reflect broader urban, tourism, and housing-market dynamics.\n",
    "\n",
    "⸻\n",
    "\n",
    "I7 — Urban pressure emerges through different mechanisms.\n",
    "The Urban Pressure Index highlights different sources of pressure across the two cities. In Rome, pressure is mainly associated with high listing concentration and professional activity. In Copenhagen, pressure is linked more to listing availability within specific neighbourhoods, despite a smaller overall market size."
   ]
  },
